WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.262
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.657 --> 00:00:06.240
JONATHAN ROCHELLE:
Welcome, everybody.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.680
It's really late, I know so
we're just going to cancel.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.150
No I'm just kidding.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.280
But I've never-- I don't
think in my life I've ever

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.860
used a lectern, but
this late I think

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.568
I'm going to be like
this the whole time.

00:00:17.568 --> 00:00:18.840
Is that all right?

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:19.830
I'm really tired.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:24.672
But I'm so excited about this
topic that I will re-energize.

00:00:24.672 --> 00:00:26.880
I'm going to talk about
machine learning and learning

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.080
technology, and just a
pre-warning in case some of you

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.860
would rather just start
going out for drinks,

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.450
I'm not going to teach you
about machine learning per se.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.330
I'm not a machine
learning expert,

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.980
but I have some
thoughts and some ideas

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.990
on how machine learning connects
to learning, human learning

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.712
that I wanted to share.

00:00:46.712 --> 00:00:48.420
And you will hopefully
learn a little bit

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.900
about machine learning if
you don't know much about it.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.540
There's a little bit there.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.600
I manage Google Apps
for Education right now,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.280
and that's what really
gets me into this topic.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.930
And I like people to
know me as I see myself.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:07.800
So I like to share
pictures that I have

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.810
in my head when I had hair.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:15.120
That's me before my
interview for my first job

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.391
that I did not get.

00:01:17.391 --> 00:01:19.890
And that's my Toyota Celica in
the background, which I still

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:20.730
love to this day.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:21.860
I don't have it anymore.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.390
But I'm a product
manager at Google.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.194
I co-founded Google
Docs and Drive.

00:01:26.194 --> 00:01:28.110
I was part of the original
team that did that.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.360
That was 11 and 1/2 years ago.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.420
I started in 2005 at Google.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.540
And now I manage G
Suite for Education

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.920
and the Jamboard product, which
you might have seen earlier.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.100
I'm also a school board member,
probably the hardest job

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.180
I've ever taken on.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.330
I knew I was taking on a job
that I might regret when,

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:53.220
instead of congratulating
me, my neighbors thanked me,

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.760
because that's really
the kind of job it is.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.810
Nobody wants it.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.502
And most importantly, I'm
the father of three students.

00:02:00.502 --> 00:02:02.460
And I think this is what
motivates me the most.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.610
This is what drives me to
care so much, so deeply

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.000
about education and improving
it, and applying technology

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.510
in a way that is
meaningful for the learner,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.890
not for the technology.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.440
And so my learning
values, I would say,

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.320
are in three main areas,
things that I really feel.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.240
First, creativity--
creativity, I think,

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.180
is just the basis for so
much and so many things

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.205
that we should learn
and should teach.

00:02:29.205 --> 00:02:30.270
Discovery of passion--

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:33.450
I feel like it's every
educator's responsibility

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.270
to help kids discover what
they might be excited about.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.640
It's also every
person's responsibility

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.710
and every parent's
responsibility

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:43.420
to do that for their
children or themselves.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.420
And learning through
engagement-- something that

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.840
is actually I would
say more possible now,

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.540
but always difficult because
engagement for one person

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.180
is different than the next.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.090
So I want to step back and look
at collaboration because that's

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:00.120
where we made some advances in
the products that I worked on.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.815
And there is a bit
of an analogy here,

00:03:01.815 --> 00:03:03.690
but collaboration 10
years ago could probably

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:05.170
be described as the following.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:07.380
First of all, it was
incredibly painful.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.570
To work together was hard.

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:14.010
There was incredible fear about
what if I lost this thing?

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:17.540
I'm working for an hour on my
machine, and I lost it, right?

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:19.290
My hard drive crashes.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.690
And that is followed by
sadness when it does happen

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.760
and perhaps anger.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.800
And then, of course, you go
through the many stages of--

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.640
and denial is always there.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.500
And I love to look
at this model and say

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.990
it's very similar in education.

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.390
There is so much of this
that happens in education,

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.990
these models that we deny
perhaps the things that

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:42.960
are wrong with it because
it's actually pretty good,

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.750
and it's better than it was.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.650
So when we got personal
productivity tools

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.780
they were so much better that we
lived with them for very long.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.159
And so when we started
the Docs efforts,

00:03:52.159 --> 00:03:53.700
we were going after
something that we

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.110
felt was very
important, and that

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:56.280
was the collaboration side.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:57.690
So we took something
like spreadsheets,

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:58.960
and that's where it started.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.189
And we said yeah, it's great.

00:04:00.189 --> 00:04:01.230
Spreadsheets are amazing.

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:04.440
Personal productivity tools were
so powerful, are so powerful,

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.560
but what was painful and
frustrating was the sharing.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.124
And so we went after that.

00:04:09.124 --> 00:04:11.040
And so we delivered
something that wasn't just

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.748
spreadsheets on the
web, but we delivered

00:04:12.748 --> 00:04:15.690
something that was shared
spreadsheets on the web.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.300
We added that-- We basically
tweaked two features.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.100
We added the share button.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.987
And more importantly, we
took away the save button.

00:04:22.987 --> 00:04:25.320
We like to say we killed the
save button because there's

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.080
no reason for us to have to
take that other mundane step

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.810
of saving when there's that
risk, that fear that you're

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:31.980
going to lose what you have.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.760
So we just looked at
something that was great,

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.051
and we tried to make it better.

00:04:35.051 --> 00:04:37.290
And this is what we ended
up with in Docs, something

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.831
that you're actually
collaborating on

00:04:38.831 --> 00:04:40.390
at the same time.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:42.450
So getting to G Suite,
these are the products

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.604
that I was involved
in from that time.

00:04:44.604 --> 00:04:46.270
I had a small company
that was acquired.

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:48.480
There was another company
acquired called Writely.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:49.920
And we built these products up--

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.650
Spreadsheets, Docs,
Slides, Forms.

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:55.200
And we were going after that
problem of collaboration.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.070
And over that time, a
tremendous number of educators

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:02.580
found what they would
call magic in those tools

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.770
because of how they
did things differently

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.290
that suddenly
transformed them into not

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:10.380
being personal productivity
tools, but being learning

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:11.075
tools--

00:05:11.075 --> 00:05:12.450
and most importantly
because they

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:14.460
were able to give
students fast feedback

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:17.112
and they were able to let
students work together.

00:05:17.112 --> 00:05:19.320
That wasn't what we expected
when we built the tools,

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:20.640
but that's what happened.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.020
And about four years ago, I
changed what I did and stopped

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:27.270
managing Docs and
Drive and moved over

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.640
to Google Apps for
Education and to move over

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:32.389
to try to build new
productivity tools.

00:05:32.389 --> 00:05:34.680
And that's when we launched
Google Classroom and Google

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.200
Expeditions, the VR tool
that's used in classrooms,

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.980
and Jamboard.

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:43.510
So that was my involvement.

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:45.730
So you look at what's
gone on in technology.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:47.560
You now have the
world's information.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.150
It's at your fingertips.

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:52.414
It's literally at your
fingertips on your phone.

00:05:52.414 --> 00:05:53.830
It's even-- perhaps
you don't even

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:56.200
need something on your
fingertips to do it, right?

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.247
You just use your voice.

00:05:57.247 --> 00:05:58.330
And you get what you need.

00:05:58.330 --> 00:05:59.163
You get the answers.

00:05:59.163 --> 00:06:00.310
The answers are all there--

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:01.427
the world's information.

00:06:01.427 --> 00:06:03.010
And then you add
something like Drive,

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:04.570
or any product like
that, and now you

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:07.162
have all of your information.

00:06:07.162 --> 00:06:08.620
So you have the
world's information

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:10.870
and your information
accessible and useful.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.660
Now what?

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:14.500
OK, that's all there.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:17.960
When you think about what
at least I did in school

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:21.245
is I learned by
memorizing some of that.

00:06:21.245 --> 00:06:22.870
The world's information
I'm memorizing.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:27.520
I'm working on memorizing, and
my kids still the 50 states'

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:29.680
capitals in the United States.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:33.220
I don't know why my kids are
memorizing the 50 states'

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:34.450
capitals.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:35.530
I really have no idea.

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:38.900
Because they can walk into my
kitchen and say OK, Google--

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:42.190
oh, sorry if your
phones all light up--

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:44.140
what's the capital of Nebraska?

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:48.754
I know it's Lincoln, by the
way, because I was taught that.

00:06:48.754 --> 00:06:49.420
So we have that.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:50.950
But now what?

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:54.130
How do we get to the
self-driving car of education?

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:58.390
How do we make it so
we take that next leap?

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:00.790
What should our students learn--

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:03.370
facts and previously
known algorithms

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:06.720
and how to do that rote step
to do, say, long division?

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.030
Or should they learn
problem-solving skills

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.580
and tools for problem
solving to improve life,

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:18.950
to actually discover and
create new things, to invent?

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:23.406
Now learning, has it changed?

00:07:23.406 --> 00:07:24.780
When you look at
how learning has

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:27.250
changed, I want to
step back and say,

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:28.620
well, what kind of learning?

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.700
Machine learning
versus human learning--

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.220
how has learning changed
in those two areas?

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:39.260
So let's look at
machine learning.

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:41.869
Let's see if we can learn
something from that.

00:07:41.869 --> 00:07:43.910
So computers are great at
following instructions.

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:45.500
It's what turned
me on to computing,

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:48.740
to computer science, when I
was a junior in high school.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:53.030
Absolutely fell in love within
two days of a computer class--

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:54.821
the called it "computer
math" back then--

00:07:54.821 --> 00:07:56.570
because I realized
that the computer would

00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:57.737
do whatever I said.

00:07:57.737 --> 00:07:59.570
I could figure out how
to give it a command,

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:00.120
and it would do it.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:00.950
It would carry it out.

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:02.491
And computers are
just great at that.

00:08:02.491 --> 00:08:04.130
You tell it what to
do, and it does it.

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:06.600
And that's great.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.229
Computers are good
at doing math.

00:08:09.229 --> 00:08:11.770
You can just ask it a question,
and it'll give you an answer.

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:13.180
That's just what it does, right?

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:16.170
That's what the computers do.

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:19.080
And remembering facts--
it's got every fact.

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:22.670
And I say computers lightly.

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:26.970
There's an easy way to get
any fact from the internet.

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:31.970
And repetitive tasks--
give it something

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:33.130
repetitive and mundane.

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:34.909
The computer will eat it up--

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:36.169
humans not so much.

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:38.658
They'll do it, but
they won't like it.

00:08:38.658 --> 00:08:41.690
Or most people-- some people do
actually like repetitive tasks.

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:42.380
That's OK.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:45.320
And then we made
computers more powerful.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:47.380
You add speed and data capacity.

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:50.040
And over the last couple
of decades, data collection

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:51.540
and machine learning
specialization,

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.170
a miniaturization in cooling
and power consumption.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.600
All those things
came together for us

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:01.380
to take a leap in computing.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.020
And that leap was
actually quite noticeable.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:08.910
And then humans were able
to teach computers because

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:11.730
of that power to do things
like recognize patterns

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:12.720
and classify data.

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.680
It's why we can point our phones
at a sign in another language

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:19.450
and get an immediate
translation.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:23.840
You can do that today,
and it's amazing.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.900
And it's natural now, the
power we have in our hands

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:29.750
in our phones because
of that miniaturization.

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:34.280
And the power and the computing,
in conjunction with the cloud,

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:35.400
gives us that.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.460
And we can take
advantage of that.

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:39.380
Clustering data,
predicting values,

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:41.470
ranking relevance--
there's so many things

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:43.220
that we were able to
teach computers to do

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:45.470
because the power was there.

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:48.960
And so when you look at machine
learning, there are two models.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:50.980
And I'm using
something lightly here

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:53.280
so don't worry
about the semantics

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:54.930
of "artificial intelligence."

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:57.420
But certainly we called
it artificial intelligence

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:59.100
for many decades.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.610
And that's basically making
machines "intelligent"

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.560
by giving them facts
and letting them

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:10.110
be able to make the next step
by saying, if this, then that.

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:12.261
Machine learning is making
machines that learn.

00:10:12.261 --> 00:10:14.760
And you could still call that
artificial intelligence, which

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:17.250
is why I'm saying don't
worry about the semantics.

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:20.382
But those are the two
modes of machine learning.

00:10:20.382 --> 00:10:21.840
But if you look at
these two things

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.290
and you want to compare
them, the old way,

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:26.040
what I'm calling AI
here even though you

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:29.700
can say AI is everything,
is just basically

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:32.040
saying, if then else,
but so many times

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:34.420
that a human could never do it.

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:37.980
So if this pattern exists,
then do this, else.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:39.450
Does this pattern exist?

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:40.560
Then do this, else.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:41.850
If this pattern exists--

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.280
and it just looks for thousands
and thousands or millions

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:46.080
of patterns and
is able to mimic.

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:48.021
And that's what I would
call an expert system.

00:10:48.021 --> 00:10:49.020
And that is the old way.

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:51.450
That's the way we used to
think about making computers

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:51.960
intelligent.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:53.270
It's the best thing we had.

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:55.020
And the new way, the
machine learning way,

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:58.890
is much more about writing
computer programs that

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:00.960
learn from examples--

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:03.630
so trying to classify
data, classify emails.

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:08.460
So it actually modifies itself
based on patterns it sees,

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.550
and by giving it answers, by
saying this answer was right

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:12.630
or this answer was wrong.

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:20.210
So if you've never
heard of Joel Benjamin,

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:24.040
you've probably heard
of one of his students.

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:26.780
And one of his
students was Deep Blue.

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:30.850
Deep Blue was the IBM
computer that learned chess

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:33.170
from Joel Benjamin.

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:37.270
Joel is an expert
at chess, and IBM

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:41.590
hired him to help teach Deep
Blue how to play chess better

00:11:41.590 --> 00:11:43.150
than any human.

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:46.970
Now if Joel Benjamin wasn't
better than every other human,

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:48.800
how did he teach that
computer to be better

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:50.090
than every other human?

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:54.490
It's because Deep Blue had
a much broader capacity.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:58.060
So Mr. Benjamin could give
it all of the knowledge

00:11:58.060 --> 00:11:59.024
through games.

00:11:59.024 --> 00:12:00.940
Yes, if this happens,
this is what I would do.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:02.648
If this happens, this
is what I would do.

00:12:02.648 --> 00:12:04.750
If this happens, this
is what I would do.

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:06.610
So it's some level
of patterning,

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:09.520
but it's mostly
an expert system.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:14.230
And in 1996, Deep Blue was
able to beat the human chess

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:14.860
champion.

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:16.660
And it was an amazing moment.

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:19.270
That was 1996.

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:26.230
20 years later, another product,
AlphaGo, using machine learning

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:28.990
was able to do something that
in 1996 everybody thought would

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:31.360
take many decades to achieve.

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:32.690
It took two decades.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:35.770
That's a long time, but not that
long in the scheme of things.

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:39.880
And that is to beat a
champion Go player--

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:43.090
does anybody here
play the game Go?

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:45.390
OK, I respect you.

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:47.400
That is a hard game.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:48.330
I don't play it.

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:50.790
It's a really hard game,
and it's very complex.

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:52.290
And I understand
why people thought,

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:57.180
no, a computer will
never beat a human at Go.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:59.910
Very, very complex,
but that happened

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:01.530
in 2016, just this past year.

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:05.460
AlphaGo was the program that was
able to beat the human player.

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:08.220
And to give you an example
of the complexity increase

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:11.100
that happened from
1996 to 2016, I just

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:13.930
want to show you a visual.

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:17.970
So if you look at
the dot on the right

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:21.730
versus the dot on
the left, that's

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:23.290
the difference in complexity.

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:26.312
The dot on the left versus
the dot on the right

00:13:26.312 --> 00:13:28.270
is the complexity difference
between the number

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:30.820
of moves in a game of chess
versus the number of moves

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:32.710
in a typical game of Go.

00:13:32.710 --> 00:13:35.020
And that means the
dot on the left

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:40.190
doesn't even justify one
pixel on that big screen.

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:41.870
That's the difference
in the complexity.

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:45.340
To put it another way, if you
look at the number of moves

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:47.680
in a game of chess,
it equals the number

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:50.870
of atoms in the human body.

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:54.080
But if you look at the number of
moves in a typical game of Go,

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:58.114
it equals the number of
atoms in the universe.

00:13:58.114 --> 00:13:59.030
That's the difference.

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:05.044
That's the leap that was taken
from '96 to 2016, 20 years.

00:14:05.044 --> 00:14:07.210
And there are different
flavors of machine learning.

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:10.600
So this is just an understanding
of what machine learning means.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:12.130
The first is
learning by example.

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:14.290
They call it
supervised learning.

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:16.000
So something like
a spam detector

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:19.414
trained on very simple examples
to say yes, this is spam.

00:14:19.414 --> 00:14:20.080
No, this is not.

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:20.860
Yes this is spam.

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:22.620
No, this is not.

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:25.700
And it's very, very effective
and is used in many machine

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:28.590
learning examples that
you see in your life,

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:30.315
including, if you use
Gmail, Gmail spam.

00:14:30.315 --> 00:14:31.190
And it's pretty good.

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:33.320
Gmail spam detection
is pretty good.

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:35.690
The next is unsupervised
learning, which

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:37.640
is more like pattern discovery.

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:40.380
And it's very hard,
but it's clustering.

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:44.090
It's a method to cluster data,
like these things look similar.

00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:45.200
These things look similar.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:47.547
These things look similar.

00:14:47.547 --> 00:14:49.880
But if you don't have examples,
that's a good way to go.

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:51.800
And then the next one,
probably the hardest,

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:53.480
is reinforcement learning.

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:56.420
And that's feedback, but
after a long string of things.

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:59.180
And that's more like when
you win the game of chess,

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:02.420
and saying, OK, that long
string of moves that happened,

00:15:02.420 --> 00:15:03.620
that worked.

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:06.290
And you have to look back
and understand why it worked.

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:07.502
So it's very slow.

00:15:07.502 --> 00:15:08.960
A lot of people in
machine learning

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:11.800
would say that might actually
be the next big thing.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:16.130
It might require the next leap
in capacity and capability.

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:18.130
And if you're really
interested in these models,

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:21.490
there's a ton of research
on research.google.com.

00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:24.370
So just some examples--
speech recognition research.

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:29.230
Googlers who do this research
share a ton of it here.

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:32.671
And so pedestrian detection
for self-driving cars--

00:15:32.671 --> 00:15:34.420
that's machine learning
because there's so

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:37.837
many patterns of
what a pedestrian is.

00:15:37.837 --> 00:15:39.920
There's movement patterns,
there's shape patterns,

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:43.120
there's things that actually
require more than just saying,

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:48.139
oh, I recognize a human, because
humans come in many forms.

00:15:48.139 --> 00:15:49.930
Things like natural
language understanding,

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:52.180
machine translation,
even advertising.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:53.510
There's so much research there.

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:57.221
So if you're interested in the
topic, definitely go there.

00:15:57.221 --> 00:15:58.220
Some of these examples--

00:15:58.220 --> 00:15:59.390
I gave Gmail spam before.

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:02.570
It uses of what they call a
feed-forward neural network.

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:05.990
And it intercepts now
99.9% of all spam.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:08.630
And every time you hit the
yes, this is spam button,

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:10.920
you're helping teach that model.

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:12.690
Thank you for doing that.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.086
You keep my inbox clear of spam.

00:16:15.086 --> 00:16:16.460
And I try to do
the same for you.

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:22.910
Speech recognition is another
example, taking acoustic input

00:16:22.910 --> 00:16:27.040
and putting that through a
deeper current neural network

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:31.240
and getting the text output,
figuring out what that text is.

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:33.640
How many people here spoke
to their phone today?

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:34.960
Did you speak to your phone?

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:36.760
I do it almost every day now--

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:40.300
if nothing else, just for
a quick reply to an SMS

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:43.084
or something like that.

00:16:43.084 --> 00:16:44.750
And reduced transcription
error is now--

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:46.870
or it is becoming
better and better.

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:49.480
Google Photo Search is one
of my favorite examples.

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:53.002
I try to be unbiased when I tell
people to use Google Photos.

00:16:53.002 --> 00:16:53.710
It's really hard.

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:55.000
They don't believe
me that I'm unbiased.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.850
But I truly believe it's one of
the best products you could use

00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:00.430
if you care about your photos
and you take too many, which

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:01.780
we all do now.

00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:03.850
I was much better
when I actually

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:07.450
had to print the photos and
get them in print at managing

00:17:07.450 --> 00:17:09.906
sharing and things,
because I only took a few

00:17:09.906 --> 00:17:12.280
and I shared those, and I
cared more about them, I think.

00:17:12.280 --> 00:17:14.530
Now I just snap
millions of photos,

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:16.810
and I don't really look
back at them that much,

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:21.030
but I started to when I started
using Google Photo Search

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:23.670
because now I can look for
things that were not tagged

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:26.700
because it actually recognizes
patterns in the photos

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:29.240
through machine learning.

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:32.390
So this has improved my life.

00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:35.530
So I can search now
for "beach," and it'll

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:38.230
find pictures of my
kids on the beach.

00:17:38.230 --> 00:17:39.160
I didn't tag these.

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:40.534
I didn't do anything
to tag them,

00:17:40.534 --> 00:17:42.910
but it can use machine learning
and the algorithms that

00:17:42.910 --> 00:17:44.076
are there to do that for me.

00:17:44.076 --> 00:17:47.080
Or "skiing"-- and I can find
pictures of my kids skiing.

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:49.900
so if I say to my mom, you got
to see your grandkids skiing,

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:52.479
I don't have to actually
think, oh let me go tag them

00:17:52.479 --> 00:17:53.020
and whatever.

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:56.590
I just do a search and
I hand her my phone.

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:58.210
The other way it
improves our lives,

00:17:58.210 --> 00:18:00.630
and maybe you guys will
recognize this, is--

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:03.680
anybody you know what that is?

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:04.910
Snapchat?

00:18:04.910 --> 00:18:07.220
Otherwise known as
Snap at this point.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:08.150
Did you buy the stock?

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:12.420
I didn't either.

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:14.679
But this improves
my life, right?

00:18:14.679 --> 00:18:16.470
Now I can sit at
breakfast with my daughter

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:18.320
and have a lot of fun.

00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:19.320
This is like literally--

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:22.150
I'm not kidding when I
say this improves my life.

00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:25.990
I'm definitely laughing
more than I used to.

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:29.400
But it actually is
something that I appreciate,

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:31.800
but obviously it's not
improving my life necessarily.

00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:32.850
It's entertainment.

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:35.400
But I will say
those same concepts

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:38.330
are improving human life.

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:41.600
And this is where I want to
make an association to learning.

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:43.940
This woman is Britney
Wenger, and in that picture

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:47.660
she's 17 years old, when
she won the Google Science

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:52.640
Fair for discovering an
incredibly effective method

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:55.320
of diagnosing breast cancer.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:19:01.110
And she did that by learning
coding, machine learning,

00:19:01.110 --> 00:19:06.770
big data, and big
data analysis, tools

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:08.720
that were not taught in school.

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:10.970
But she took it
upon herself, not

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:12.980
because she wanted to
win the science fair,

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:16.650
but because she wanted
to improve life.

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:19.660
And that's truly impactful, a
little bit more than Snapchat.

00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:25.920
So we've done an amazing job
teaching machines, really

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:28.814
amazing job, and it has
helped us as humans.

00:19:28.814 --> 00:19:29.730
But what about humans?

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:31.789
How has learning changed
over those 20 years?

00:19:31.789 --> 00:19:33.330
We saw how much it's
changed, and who

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:36.660
knows what it's going to
do over the next 20 years?

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:41.640
Somebody asked me today, do you
think your 10-year-old daughter

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:43.800
will get a driver's license?

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.837
And of course at first I'm
thinking, why wouldn't she?

00:19:46.837 --> 00:19:48.420
And then I realized
what he was asking

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:52.140
me was will she need to drive?

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:55.400
Or will self-driving
cars be the norm?

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:58.220
If they're truly better,
why are we driving?

00:19:58.220 --> 00:20:00.950
And he's in San
Francisco, and he said,

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:04.190
you ever notice how
many cars are parked

00:20:04.190 --> 00:20:06.530
on both sides of every street?

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:07.790
Imagine if that wasn't so.

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:09.780
Imagine all the
room we would gain.

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:10.930
Imagine the efficiency.

00:20:10.930 --> 00:20:13.760
Imagine the change in pollution.

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:15.740
Everything changes,
when you think about it,

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:19.310
if humans don't have to drive,
park, do all those things.

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:25.310
And literally the accident rate
goes way down because humans

00:20:25.310 --> 00:20:27.920
are really bad drivers.

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:31.650
We don't set a high bar for
machines, let's just say.

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:34.320
But if you compare human
learning to machine

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:38.320
learning, and this is just a
visual, not necessarily fact.

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:40.470
I don't have numbers
to prove this chart.

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:42.600
But where have we
gone in 20 years?

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:44.730
How have we changed
learning in 20 years?

00:20:44.730 --> 00:20:48.600
I think it's improved, but it
has not improved or changed

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:49.890
enough.

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:52.410
And it has not improved greatly.

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:54.270
it's not noticeable.

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:55.660
I can't really show it.

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:57.240
There are instances I can show.

00:20:57.240 --> 00:21:01.170
I've seen amazing
educational instances--

00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:06.120
people, situations,
classes, methods.

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:08.281
But they feel like
they're spot changes.

00:21:08.281 --> 00:21:09.780
And where is that
graph going to go?

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:12.381
How are we going to
change that graph?

00:21:12.381 --> 00:21:13.880
I want to give you
an example that I

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:16.010
feel really proves
the point of something

00:21:16.010 --> 00:21:19.400
that I think is
wrong with education.

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:21.900
This is a math problem.

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:26.040
There are 26 goats and
10 sheep on a boat.

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:27.930
And the question that's
asked is, how old

00:21:27.930 --> 00:21:30.280
is the captain of the boat?

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:32.980
This was a question asked
in the study of a bunch

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:34.510
of fifth graders not long ago.

00:21:37.130 --> 00:21:41.150
75% of the fifth graders
asked gave a numeric answer

00:21:41.150 --> 00:21:42.930
to this question.

00:21:42.930 --> 00:21:44.490
They actually
answered the question.

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:47.460
They tried to answer it.

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:50.850
Sometimes I like to give away a
Jamboard or something to person

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:52.680
anybody who can get the answer.

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:54.907
Because I don't think
there is an answer.

00:21:54.907 --> 00:21:56.740
These are the answers
the kids gave, though.

00:21:56.740 --> 00:21:59.640
75 percent, most of
them, gave the answer 36.

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:04.550
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:04.550 --> 00:22:07.730
Clearly the captain
of the boat is 36.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:09.140
And you know why?

00:22:09.140 --> 00:22:10.280
Because I have two numbers.

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:14.360
When they were asked, why do
you think the captain is 36?

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:17.210
Well, in these
types of problems,

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:19.140
we usually have to
add the two numbers.

00:22:21.860 --> 00:22:23.899
16 is another answer they give.

00:22:23.899 --> 00:22:25.690
You know, sometimes
it's tricky, and you've

00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:26.870
got to subtract them.

00:22:26.870 --> 00:22:30.030
And then there was one
or two instances of 260,

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:33.346
where they're doing
advanced math and thinking,

00:22:33.346 --> 00:22:35.220
well, I might not have
learned that in school

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:36.470
yet, but multiplication--

00:22:36.470 --> 00:22:38.680
I can do this.

00:22:38.680 --> 00:22:40.160
But this is the proof, right?

00:22:40.160 --> 00:22:41.000
They're programed.

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:42.260
This is the irony.

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:46.220
Our kids are programmed, even
though they haven't really

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:49.850
advanced as much as
machines have improved

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:55.020
in their learning, because we
teach them with rote methods.

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:57.330
We teach them to do
things in rote ways.

00:22:57.330 --> 00:23:00.970
Follow steps, not
solve problems,

00:23:00.970 --> 00:23:03.990
and not to understand.

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:05.824
And what can we do
to help solve that?

00:23:05.824 --> 00:23:06.990
What are some of the things?

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:08.430
And these are some basic things.

00:23:08.430 --> 00:23:11.220
But first of all, we could
help train teachers more,

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:12.409
give them the tools.

00:23:12.409 --> 00:23:13.950
Some of this happens
because teachers

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:15.870
are teaching the way they
were taught to teach.

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:17.536
And our teaching
programs haven't really

00:23:17.536 --> 00:23:19.260
changed that much,
or haven't changed

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:22.180
enough to include
today's tools, new tools,

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:25.340
new ways of teaching.

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:28.010
And giving children and
students and people--

00:23:28.010 --> 00:23:29.750
this is not just about kids--

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:32.030
experiences, not just facts.

00:23:32.030 --> 00:23:33.680
Let's let them
experience something.

00:23:33.680 --> 00:23:37.040
Like I said before, with
some of the data model,

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:40.670
the machine learning, we're
giving the machine experiences,

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:41.570
saying try this.

00:23:41.570 --> 00:23:42.320
Oh, fail.

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:43.850
OK, now we told you it failed.

00:23:43.850 --> 00:23:44.570
Try again.

00:23:44.570 --> 00:23:45.430
Success!

00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:46.490
Try again, fail.

00:23:46.490 --> 00:23:48.080
Try again, success.

00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:50.810
You can't get that necessarily
unless you have experiences.

00:23:50.810 --> 00:23:52.720
And there are methods
of giving experiences.

00:23:52.720 --> 00:23:55.160
I even show Google
Expeditions here.

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:58.660
It's a precursor to
something I'll show later.

00:23:58.660 --> 00:24:00.740
And experts help, too.

00:24:00.740 --> 00:24:03.920
Connect experts with
students, with learners.

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:07.880
Let them experience with
that expert has experienced.

00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:11.630
And I show basically Hangout,
Skype, Twitter as examples

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:15.350
of the technology that's there
to help with experiences.

00:24:15.350 --> 00:24:17.350
Sometimes it's just
social networks.

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:19.370
Like, sure, they're
useful in some ways.

00:24:19.370 --> 00:24:21.440
But they're useful to
connect you to experts.

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:23.690
And educators can actually
reach out on those networks

00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:25.850
to find experts when
they are not the expert.

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:29.880
The teacher just has to be
the guide, not the expert.

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:32.100
And games can help, too.

00:24:32.100 --> 00:24:35.220
Games help a tremendous amount
because they motivate learning.

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:37.950
They help to motivate learning
for everyone, not just

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:39.030
students.

00:24:39.030 --> 00:24:40.870
And I just have a quick example.

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:44.580
I have a friend
who's a driver who's

00:24:44.580 --> 00:24:47.640
not so good at driving, one
of these examples of setting

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:49.850
a low bar.

00:24:49.850 --> 00:24:54.490
But then she got a
Prius, and the Prius

00:24:54.490 --> 00:24:59.100
gives immediate
feedback and a score.

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:02.800
And as soon as you see a
score, everything changes.

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:04.660
I want a better score.

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:07.220
Slow down.

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:09.080
OK, I slow down, I
get a better score.

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:10.280
Oh my gosh, I'm learning.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:12.290
And it happens immediately.

00:25:12.290 --> 00:25:14.397
I actually did this
in my car recently.

00:25:14.397 --> 00:25:15.980
I just changed what
my dashboard would

00:25:15.980 --> 00:25:19.220
show to show my efficiency,
my fuel efficiency,

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:20.960
and it's a gas
car, unfortunately.

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:23.990
But I found myself
driving so well.

00:25:23.990 --> 00:25:28.370
I was so careful to
not accelerate too much

00:25:28.370 --> 00:25:30.905
and to not necessarily
overuse the brake.

00:25:30.905 --> 00:25:33.530
And that means I'm going to keep
a bigger distance because then

00:25:33.530 --> 00:25:34.617
I can be more efficient.

00:25:34.617 --> 00:25:36.950
And I started playing a game
to see how high I could get

00:25:36.950 --> 00:25:39.110
my miles per gallon efficiency.

00:25:39.110 --> 00:25:41.390
And not only was it fun,
but the people in the car

00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:43.460
were like, wow, you're
driving so carefully.

00:25:43.460 --> 00:25:44.690
What happened?

00:25:44.690 --> 00:25:47.270
And it really was
an obvious change.

00:25:47.270 --> 00:25:52.520
And then my son, I talked to
him from where I was driving to,

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:56.030
and he said, what
was the efficiency?

00:25:56.030 --> 00:25:57.680
What was your score
on the way down?

00:25:57.680 --> 00:25:58.640
He even cared.

00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:02.060
It was just a game
because it was fun.

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:07.694
And grades could reflect just
learning, not everything else.

00:26:07.694 --> 00:26:09.860
So it is important to know
if you're right or wrong.

00:26:09.860 --> 00:26:11.485
We said experiences
are important.

00:26:11.485 --> 00:26:12.860
And to know whether
or not it was

00:26:12.860 --> 00:26:14.420
the right answer
or wrong answer,

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:16.650
sometimes it is important
to get that feedback.

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:20.090
But we so often incorporate
everything else into a grade

00:26:20.090 --> 00:26:21.920
that we give to a student.

00:26:21.920 --> 00:26:22.920
It's like what happened?

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:24.628
I thought you really
knew your chemistry.

00:26:24.628 --> 00:26:26.250
Well, I was late on
three assignments.

00:26:26.250 --> 00:26:27.450
It's like, oh, so
we don't really

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:28.699
know if you learned chemistry.

00:26:28.699 --> 00:26:29.590
No.

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:30.454
Well, that's a pity.

00:26:30.454 --> 00:26:31.620
Oh and I was late four days.

00:26:31.620 --> 00:26:34.950
I had an A+, but I was late
four days, so I got a B.

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:35.850
There's a good idea.

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:41.037
So what are we doing in
learning technology today?

00:26:41.037 --> 00:26:43.120
And I would say learning
technology today has been

00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:44.661
focused, and there
are some instances

00:26:44.661 --> 00:26:46.760
that this is not true, and
they are more advanced.

00:26:46.760 --> 00:26:49.030
They're just not there enough.

00:26:49.030 --> 00:26:50.480
They're not everywhere.

00:26:50.480 --> 00:26:52.480
But what we're doing
generally in learning today

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:56.170
is focusing on the basics.

00:26:56.170 --> 00:26:59.230
So one thing we know
is amazing in education

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:00.180
is immediate feedback.

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:01.910
Teacher-student feedback
is so important.

00:27:01.910 --> 00:27:03.980
That's why we saw
something like Docs,

00:27:03.980 --> 00:27:08.750
and why I started with that
being so impactful for teachers

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:11.420
because they realized they could
give immediate feedback to 100

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:13.340
students without being there.

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:16.592
But what was frustrating them
was the ability to share.

00:27:16.592 --> 00:27:18.300
And that's actually
why-- in other words,

00:27:18.300 --> 00:27:19.258
they have 100 students.

00:27:19.258 --> 00:27:21.980
How do I share to 100
students and get that feedback

00:27:21.980 --> 00:27:24.050
to 100 students quickly?

00:27:24.050 --> 00:27:25.100
And it was super hard.

00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:26.950
That's why we created Classroom.

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:29.630
Google Classroom was created
on that foundation to say,

00:27:29.630 --> 00:27:31.370
if we could get
them there faster,

00:27:31.370 --> 00:27:32.990
they'll be better teachers.

00:27:32.990 --> 00:27:34.040
They'll be happier.

00:27:34.040 --> 00:27:35.840
They'll spend more
time with students,

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:37.474
and they won't be
so inefficient.

00:27:37.474 --> 00:27:39.890
We tried to get them off paper
and onto something that was

00:27:39.890 --> 00:27:43.050
easy to give feedback faster.

00:27:43.050 --> 00:27:44.510
Same thing with field trips.

00:27:44.510 --> 00:27:47.936
Field trips are
amazing experiences.

00:27:47.936 --> 00:27:50.310
It's the kind of thing that
when you look back at school,

00:27:50.310 --> 00:27:52.200
and you think about
what your remembered,

00:27:52.200 --> 00:27:53.370
field trips are memorable.

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:55.590
And it's not just the noisy bus.

00:27:55.590 --> 00:27:58.240
That's memorable in a bad way.

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:02.350
But it's incredibly enriching.

00:28:02.350 --> 00:28:03.190
You really feel it.

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:04.689
And there is some
proof and research

00:28:04.689 --> 00:28:07.030
that shows that when
your body is involved,

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:09.340
when your senses are
involved, you remember more,

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:10.310
you learn more.

00:28:10.310 --> 00:28:11.502
You're open to learning.

00:28:11.502 --> 00:28:13.210
But what's frustrating
about that is it's

00:28:13.210 --> 00:28:16.380
hard to go on field
trips, really hard.

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:17.560
They're expensive.

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:19.930
Not every kid has the
same opportunities.

00:28:19.930 --> 00:28:21.400
I've seen in my
own schools, we've

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:24.486
cut back on field trips for cost
and just for access reasons.

00:28:24.486 --> 00:28:26.360
You don't have enough
children participating,

00:28:26.360 --> 00:28:27.550
so you can't really do it.

00:28:27.550 --> 00:28:29.950
And you're usually limited
to where your bus can go,

00:28:29.950 --> 00:28:32.200
even if it's a three-hour ride.

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:38.540
And that's why we created
Google Expeditions specifically

00:28:38.540 --> 00:28:41.600
out of the team that's
focused on education,

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:45.710
so that we could at least try to
give children more experiences

00:28:45.710 --> 00:28:47.540
using virtual reality.

00:28:47.540 --> 00:28:50.690
And now it's easy to argue
that that's not the same,

00:28:50.690 --> 00:28:52.390
but it's pretty good.

00:28:52.390 --> 00:28:54.140
And we know it's pretty
good because we've

00:28:54.140 --> 00:28:56.579
tried it in hundreds and
hundreds of classrooms.

00:28:56.579 --> 00:28:58.370
And especially, even
before we launched it,

00:28:58.370 --> 00:29:00.230
we tried it with some
teachers and students,

00:29:00.230 --> 00:29:02.990
and invariably the
experience was amazing

00:29:02.990 --> 00:29:05.070
and the engagement
was super high.

00:29:05.070 --> 00:29:07.790
And when we combine that
with good lesson planning,

00:29:07.790 --> 00:29:11.270
it's really taking off in a
way that is great for students

00:29:11.270 --> 00:29:13.870
and great for teachers.

00:29:13.870 --> 00:29:16.020
And there's hundreds
and hundreds

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:19.620
of expeditions now being used in
classrooms as part of lessons,

00:29:19.620 --> 00:29:22.260
and the children are
experiencing more.

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:23.550
They can go to Rome.

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:25.320
They can go to the
Great Wall of China.

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:28.650
And it can be for a math class,
not just a history class.

00:29:28.650 --> 00:29:30.400
It's not just about
getting to that place.

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:34.317
It's about learning
using that place.

00:29:34.317 --> 00:29:36.650
Whiteboards-- and it's funny
because I never really made

00:29:36.650 --> 00:29:38.900
this connection until I
saw what was happening,

00:29:38.900 --> 00:29:41.510
but whiteboards
could be amazing.

00:29:41.510 --> 00:29:44.000
But again, there's things
that would protect the teacher

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:46.416
from using it in the way we
would like to see them use it.

00:29:46.416 --> 00:29:48.500
There's no save or
recall, no sharing.

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:51.350
You can't add imagery
necessarily to a white board,

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:54.270
unless you're an
incredible artist.

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:55.890
It's not legible.

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:59.350
You've got ink and all
the things that go wrong.

00:29:59.350 --> 00:30:00.460
So we produced Jamboard.

00:30:00.460 --> 00:30:03.400
That was really for a
business environment,

00:30:03.400 --> 00:30:05.180
but we're seeing and
learning obviously,

00:30:05.180 --> 00:30:06.810
and this is not a
new concept, it's

00:30:06.810 --> 00:30:09.570
something that really enhances
learning if you can use it

00:30:09.570 --> 00:30:10.670
the way it should be used.

00:30:13.740 --> 00:30:16.690
And that's what we're doing
in learning technology.

00:30:16.690 --> 00:30:19.410
But what could we do
if we really focused?

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:22.770
What could we do to enhance
learning using technology

00:30:22.770 --> 00:30:25.460
and advanced technology?

00:30:25.460 --> 00:30:28.460
First of all, there's an
incredible number of tools.

00:30:28.460 --> 00:30:31.467
I mentioned Brittany Wenger,
who's now a researcher.

00:30:31.467 --> 00:30:33.050
I think she's still
with Georgia Tech,

00:30:33.050 --> 00:30:37.480
but now a researcher, a medical
researcher in her 20s now.

00:30:37.480 --> 00:30:39.980
So it was I think it was 2012
that she won the science fair,

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:41.954
if you want to look that up.

00:30:41.954 --> 00:30:43.370
But there are tools
out there now,

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:44.744
and you saw probably
some of them

00:30:44.744 --> 00:30:48.810
announced today and
produced even before today.

00:30:48.810 --> 00:30:55.490
TensorFlow, natural language
APIs, big cloud data,

00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:59.720
analytics, Vision API
to pull information out

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:02.370
of videos, something that
would take just forever

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:04.694
if you had to do it manually.

00:31:04.694 --> 00:31:06.110
And maybe there's
many things that

00:31:06.110 --> 00:31:09.840
can be done if students are
taught some of these tools.

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:12.530
And if you're thinking, yeah,
but what student could actually

00:31:12.530 --> 00:31:13.280
learn those tools?

00:31:13.280 --> 00:31:14.279
Isn't that too advanced?

00:31:14.279 --> 00:31:16.740
The answer is no, they're
not too advanced anymore.

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:19.580
The bar for using those
tools has come way down.

00:31:19.580 --> 00:31:21.980
And what we often
do is we project

00:31:21.980 --> 00:31:24.830
what we feel is our own
limitation on our students.

00:31:24.830 --> 00:31:26.930
And we think, wow,
I don't get that.

00:31:26.930 --> 00:31:29.540
That doesn't mean the
student won't get it.

00:31:29.540 --> 00:31:34.340
I found that my 16-year-old
learns much faster than I do.

00:31:34.340 --> 00:31:36.650
And he'll get things
before me now.

00:31:36.650 --> 00:31:38.120
And I actually
remember that moment

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:42.650
with my dad, when he was
frustrated with me passing him

00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:44.200
in my ability to do things.

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:47.220
And it always made me
feel good to teach him.

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:48.970
And now I'm lucky
because I feel good when

00:31:48.970 --> 00:31:50.560
my son is able to teach me.

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:52.990
But we have to give them the
benefit of the doubt, which

00:31:52.990 --> 00:31:56.440
means letting them learn things
that are more advanced than we

00:31:56.440 --> 00:31:59.020
knew and what we were taught.

00:31:59.020 --> 00:32:01.450
What we learned in
school is not what

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:03.460
we should be teaching
our students in school.

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:05.439
It's just not the same thing.

00:32:05.439 --> 00:32:07.480
And unfortunately, that's
not where we are today.

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:10.750
We're basically teaching
them what we were taught.

00:32:10.750 --> 00:32:12.280
So these tools are
actually useful.

00:32:12.280 --> 00:32:13.946
And imagine how many
Brittany Wengers we

00:32:13.946 --> 00:32:16.600
could have if we taught
everyone those tools

00:32:16.600 --> 00:32:18.192
that she had to go
learn on her own.

00:32:18.192 --> 00:32:19.900
She had to even know
they were available.

00:32:23.254 --> 00:32:25.170
One of the things we
could do with those tools

00:32:25.170 --> 00:32:29.300
is match learning
to the learner.

00:32:29.300 --> 00:32:31.580
I like to say if you've
seen one student,

00:32:31.580 --> 00:32:35.892
you've seen one student, but
we teach them all the same.

00:32:35.892 --> 00:32:37.600
And I know that's not
true in every case.

00:32:37.600 --> 00:32:38.850
Sometimes there are breakouts.

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:41.807
There is a way to group
children or give them

00:32:41.807 --> 00:32:43.390
personalized learning
with some tools.

00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:45.580
There are some amazing
tools out there.

00:32:45.580 --> 00:32:49.370
Like I said, they're just
not pervasive enough.

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:52.020
But we could do a lot more
if we'd collect the data.

00:32:52.020 --> 00:32:54.530
So first we have to recognize
what data should we collect,

00:32:54.530 --> 00:32:57.140
and then we have to say, what
can we do with that data?

00:32:57.140 --> 00:32:59.720
But this is one thing that we
can make incredible advances

00:32:59.720 --> 00:33:00.771
on if we really focus.

00:33:00.771 --> 00:33:02.270
And there's three
areas that I would

00:33:02.270 --> 00:33:04.400
say you could match the
learning to the learner.

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:05.340
First is the content.

00:33:05.340 --> 00:33:06.740
What are you actually
using to teach?

00:33:06.740 --> 00:33:07.450
Then the format.

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:08.870
How is it delivered?

00:33:08.870 --> 00:33:09.560
Is it physical?

00:33:09.560 --> 00:33:10.559
You're out in the field.

00:33:10.559 --> 00:33:12.740
Or is it video,
or is it reading?

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:16.790
I know myself and my wife, we
learn in very different ways.

00:33:16.790 --> 00:33:20.450
She's a reader, and
I lose consciousness

00:33:20.450 --> 00:33:24.650
when I read basically.

00:33:24.650 --> 00:33:26.810
I'm a visual learner.

00:33:26.810 --> 00:33:29.060
And the pace, the pace
is always different.

00:33:29.060 --> 00:33:32.480
But a slow-paced learner
is not a worse learner.

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:34.130
They are just a
slower-paced learner.

00:33:34.130 --> 00:33:35.240
They might absorb more.

00:33:37.787 --> 00:33:39.370
The other thing we
could do is provide

00:33:39.370 --> 00:33:40.456
more immediate feedback.

00:33:40.456 --> 00:33:42.580
And here I want to give
you an example of something

00:33:42.580 --> 00:33:43.470
that actually exists.

00:33:43.470 --> 00:33:45.220
It was launched very recently.

00:33:45.220 --> 00:33:47.560
And I just took a few
screen shots, just earlier

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:50.320
today, to show what
can be done with this.

00:33:50.320 --> 00:33:52.855
It's called the Perspective API.

00:33:52.855 --> 00:33:55.230
And when you leave here today,
or if you have your laptop

00:33:55.230 --> 00:33:57.180
and you're really
bored, you can actually

00:33:57.180 --> 00:33:59.410
go to the Perspective
API and try this.

00:33:59.410 --> 00:34:01.980
There's a lot of things
you can try there.

00:34:01.980 --> 00:34:04.800
One of the things you can
try struck me immediately

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:06.420
as something that
can be applied,

00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:09.100
and not just
education generally,

00:34:09.100 --> 00:34:12.630
but specifically
educating people,

00:34:12.630 --> 00:34:15.900
and especially students, how to
communicate more effectively.

00:34:15.900 --> 00:34:18.239
What could be more
important than helping

00:34:18.239 --> 00:34:20.460
us communicate more
effectively with each other?

00:34:20.460 --> 00:34:23.690
How often do you think, wow,
I can't deal with that person.

00:34:23.690 --> 00:34:26.310
Or that was such a
tough discussion.

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:27.900
Or, wow, did you see that email?

00:34:27.900 --> 00:34:29.250
Can you believe that?

00:34:29.250 --> 00:34:31.230
I can't believe that
was the response.

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:33.659
Or the reply all that
you wish the person could

00:34:33.659 --> 00:34:36.510
read before they sent.

00:34:36.510 --> 00:34:40.560
The Perspective API was
launched out of the Jigsaw team

00:34:40.560 --> 00:34:41.790
at Google.

00:34:41.790 --> 00:34:44.670
And Jigsaw used to be called
the Google Ideas team.

00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:49.530
And they have very high
I would say aspirations

00:34:49.530 --> 00:34:52.556
to help humanity, literally.

00:34:52.556 --> 00:34:54.389
And one of the things
they were trying to do

00:34:54.389 --> 00:34:57.730
is reduce toxicity
in communication.

00:34:57.730 --> 00:34:59.460
And so if immediately
you're thinking,

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:01.904
oh, you mean YouTube comments?

00:35:01.904 --> 00:35:02.820
You're actually right.

00:35:02.820 --> 00:35:06.070
That's one of the things that
they actually tested it on.

00:35:06.070 --> 00:35:09.960
But others, like the New
York Times and Wikipedia,

00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:10.860
they tried it.

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:14.790
And they tried to train that
model to measure toxicity.

00:35:14.790 --> 00:35:16.920
And they have a
tool online that I

00:35:16.920 --> 00:35:19.740
would love to see wired to
every communication method

00:35:19.740 --> 00:35:22.110
that we use, even Snapchat.

00:35:22.110 --> 00:35:23.320
And I'll give you an example.

00:35:23.320 --> 00:35:27.540
If I said, "you are so stupid"
in response to somebody,

00:35:27.540 --> 00:35:31.170
that gets a 97%
toxicity rating--

00:35:31.170 --> 00:35:33.132
97%.

00:35:33.132 --> 00:35:34.590
But I bet that
comment is out there

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:36.440
in the ether a million times.

00:35:36.440 --> 00:35:39.320
You are so stupid.

00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:43.460
And basically it's saying
97% similar to comments

00:35:43.460 --> 00:35:45.335
people said were toxic.

00:35:45.335 --> 00:35:47.210
So they had people, just
like the spam thing,

00:35:47.210 --> 00:35:50.024
say yep, toxic, toxic,
toxic, not so toxic.

00:35:50.024 --> 00:35:51.440
You know, how would
you rate this?

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:54.440
And that model was
trained against this data.

00:35:54.440 --> 00:36:00.560
If I just change that to "that
is so stupid," not you, that,

00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:01.920
it goes down a little bit--

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:03.170
94 percent.

00:36:03.170 --> 00:36:04.730
All right, not quite enough.

00:36:04.730 --> 00:36:06.620
I should be a little
less toxic still.

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:09.910
But if I change it again
to say "that is so dumb,"

00:36:09.910 --> 00:36:12.580
now that gets a 76%
toxicity rating.

00:36:12.580 --> 00:36:14.590
I'm learning.

00:36:14.590 --> 00:36:17.176
I'm learning to be less toxic.

00:36:17.176 --> 00:36:18.550
And then if I
change that to say,

00:36:18.550 --> 00:36:22.077
"that might be a bad idea,"
that goes down to 12%.

00:36:22.077 --> 00:36:24.660
And if I go even further and say
I want to be even less toxic,

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:26.440
I say "that is not
a great idea,"--

00:36:26.440 --> 00:36:28.225
2%.

00:36:28.225 --> 00:36:28.725
Send.

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:34.790
Imagine if every student,
instead of saying, no,

00:36:34.790 --> 00:36:38.900
you cannot use chat, or
you cannot use email--

00:36:38.900 --> 00:36:40.280
how many schools
shut down email?

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:42.140
They say no, the students
can't send email.

00:36:42.140 --> 00:36:43.850
They can't talk to
each other on email.

00:36:43.850 --> 00:36:46.249
A lot of schools, if you're
not involved in education,

00:36:46.249 --> 00:36:48.290
a lot of schools basically
say, we don't actually

00:36:48.290 --> 00:36:50.623
give our kids email at school,
especially in the younger

00:36:50.623 --> 00:36:52.730
grades.

00:36:52.730 --> 00:36:55.130
But imagine if we did
and we helped them learn

00:36:55.130 --> 00:36:56.750
how to communicate better.

00:36:56.750 --> 00:36:58.850
How many bad adult
communications

00:36:58.850 --> 00:37:00.890
could you avoid in the future?

00:37:03.782 --> 00:37:05.240
So that's just an
idea of something

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:06.800
you can do with Perspective API.

00:37:06.800 --> 00:37:08.720
And there's so many
other things out there

00:37:08.720 --> 00:37:09.570
that you could do that with.

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:11.120
There are so many
other applications.

00:37:11.120 --> 00:37:13.703
And if you think through some
of the things that are announced

00:37:13.703 --> 00:37:19.700
here or at the next industry API
conference or machine learning

00:37:19.700 --> 00:37:21.230
conference, there
are so many ways

00:37:21.230 --> 00:37:23.229
you can look at those
things and say, how can we

00:37:23.229 --> 00:37:24.210
use that in education?

00:37:24.210 --> 00:37:26.460
How could a learner use that?

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:28.830
And I would compel you to
do that because there's also

00:37:28.830 --> 00:37:30.590
opportunity there.

00:37:30.590 --> 00:37:33.135
But if I look at
learning technology,

00:37:33.135 --> 00:37:34.510
I like to think
of it as there is

00:37:34.510 --> 00:37:37.910
the now, learning technology,
and that's where we've been.

00:37:37.910 --> 00:37:39.890
So providing
engaging experiences,

00:37:39.890 --> 00:37:43.610
reducing teaching
friction, and relevance

00:37:43.610 --> 00:37:45.480
for students futures--
in other words,

00:37:45.480 --> 00:37:48.140
learning technology, the
stuff that we use to learn,

00:37:48.140 --> 00:37:51.350
is relevant because
kids will use it.

00:37:51.350 --> 00:37:54.050
If you look at the verb
learning technology,

00:37:54.050 --> 00:37:56.870
you want to learn technology,
which also means teach

00:37:56.870 --> 00:38:01.460
technology, if teachers
model modern practices,

00:38:01.460 --> 00:38:03.310
students will learn it better.

00:38:03.310 --> 00:38:07.630
If they see a teacher handing
out paper, collecting paper,

00:38:07.630 --> 00:38:10.150
and taking a week
to give feedback,

00:38:10.150 --> 00:38:12.880
that is not a good model
for a student to think, oh,

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:14.590
that's how it works.

00:38:14.590 --> 00:38:15.175
No it doesn't.

00:38:15.175 --> 00:38:18.180
It doesn't work
like that at all.

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:21.080
And if they're forced to sit
there, every single student,

00:38:21.080 --> 00:38:23.270
and learn cursive
writing, and feel

00:38:23.270 --> 00:38:25.640
free to come up here and
attack me later about cursive

00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:29.830
writing if you want,
I'll argue till I'm done.

00:38:29.830 --> 00:38:33.550
Every single student
learning cursive writing--

00:38:33.550 --> 00:38:35.860
I would argue that
that's not necessarily

00:38:35.860 --> 00:38:37.630
relevant to their future.

00:38:37.630 --> 00:38:41.380
It might be if
they're an artist.

00:38:41.380 --> 00:38:44.050
But teachers modeling
modern practice

00:38:44.050 --> 00:38:46.570
is important for
learning technology.

00:38:46.570 --> 00:38:49.270
In other words, learn it.

00:38:49.270 --> 00:38:51.650
And discovering advanced tools--

00:38:51.650 --> 00:38:52.180
learn it.

00:38:52.180 --> 00:38:53.950
Learn those tools.

00:38:53.950 --> 00:38:57.150
Teach those tools.

00:38:57.150 --> 00:38:59.760
And then learning how to
apply that technology.

00:38:59.760 --> 00:39:01.500
How should I use it?

00:39:01.500 --> 00:39:03.000
Using relevant
examples and saying,

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:05.930
this is how we use
this technology.

00:39:05.930 --> 00:39:07.430
I want my kids to
learn spreadsheets

00:39:07.430 --> 00:39:11.330
in the third grade or the second
grade so that they understand,

00:39:11.330 --> 00:39:12.790
oh, yeah, that's
how you do that.

00:39:12.790 --> 00:39:14.240
That's how you actually--

00:39:14.240 --> 00:39:16.312
first of all,
that's what data is.

00:39:16.312 --> 00:39:18.020
They don't even know
what data is usually

00:39:18.020 --> 00:39:21.312
when they get to, say,
the ninth grade sometimes.

00:39:21.312 --> 00:39:23.020
And it doesn't matter
what tool they use.

00:39:23.020 --> 00:39:25.510
If they really want to
use Excel, I'll allow it.

00:39:25.510 --> 00:39:28.960
But if they want to use Google
Spreadsheets, I'd be happier.

00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:30.680
But I'm just giving
them as an example.

00:39:30.680 --> 00:39:32.642
Spreadsheets are super
useful in our lives.

00:39:32.642 --> 00:39:34.600
They should learn it
super early, and they can.

00:39:34.600 --> 00:39:36.022
Trust me, I've seen it.

00:39:36.022 --> 00:39:38.230
First graders, second graders,
third graders learning

00:39:38.230 --> 00:39:39.940
spreadsheets is totally doable.

00:39:39.940 --> 00:39:42.812
And that's just one
example, a simple one,

00:39:42.812 --> 00:39:44.020
but learning how to apply it.

00:39:44.020 --> 00:39:45.450
But I would say you
go to the next level.

00:39:45.450 --> 00:39:47.620
And what we're at today,
what we're trying to do,

00:39:47.620 --> 00:39:50.524
is improve and just
make learning better.

00:39:50.524 --> 00:39:52.690
And to get better learning,
we take those two things

00:39:52.690 --> 00:39:54.640
and we go to the
next step and just

00:39:54.640 --> 00:39:58.330
basically focus on learning
to solve unknown problems,

00:39:58.330 --> 00:40:01.450
not learning to follow the
examples of our predecessors

00:40:01.450 --> 00:40:03.040
that solve problems
and say, oh, yeah,

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:04.840
this is how this
problem was solved.

00:40:04.840 --> 00:40:05.770
Follow these steps.

00:40:05.770 --> 00:40:07.745
Learn these steps.

00:40:07.745 --> 00:40:09.120
Maybe there's a
new way to do it.

00:40:13.820 --> 00:40:15.770
And learning to learn--

00:40:15.770 --> 00:40:19.700
figuring out what do you do
when you don't know something.

00:40:19.700 --> 00:40:23.060
And trying not to just answer
students with, oh, here's

00:40:23.060 --> 00:40:24.150
the answer.

00:40:24.150 --> 00:40:25.981
This is the answer.

00:40:25.981 --> 00:40:26.480
No.

00:40:26.480 --> 00:40:29.450
Here's how you go about
finding an answer.

00:40:29.450 --> 00:40:30.950
So if we don't
allow our students

00:40:30.950 --> 00:40:35.340
to use their phones
in school, they

00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:37.980
don't know that they could look
for that answer much quicker

00:40:37.980 --> 00:40:39.180
much more quickly.

00:40:39.180 --> 00:40:40.680
And they don't know
that that's just

00:40:40.680 --> 00:40:42.030
a fact that they could look up.

00:40:42.030 --> 00:40:44.010
Why don't we let them do that?

00:40:44.010 --> 00:40:47.310
We find value in them
memorizing that fact instead?

00:40:47.310 --> 00:40:48.990
I don't.

00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:51.400
So I want to see us
teaching students to learn.

00:40:54.334 --> 00:40:55.250
And I'll end it there.

00:40:55.250 --> 00:40:56.083
Thank you very much.

00:41:01.730 --> 00:41:05.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

